From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HOTTY Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:25:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 19/21] termio: set the pty size after the grid's, under the
 renderer lock

Termio.resize set the pty size (TIOCSWINSZ, so the child gets SIGWINCH)
before it took the renderer lock and resized the terminal. The read
thread parses the child's output under that lock, so under load the
frame a full screen app drew for the new size could be parsed onto the
old grid. The resize then moved it:
- a shorter screen trims rows from the top once its bottom rows hold
  text, so the frame slid up a row;
- a wider frame wrapped, and its last row scrolled the screen.
Images anchored to those rows moved with them, and what the app drew
stayed wrong until its next frame.

Resize the terminal first, then set the pty size while still holding
the lock. Whatever the child writes in answer to SIGWINCH is parsed
only once the grid has the size it was written for. The in-band size
report (mode 2048) still follows the resize, as before. The ioctl is
cheap, so the critical section stays small.

Reported by plexos (RESIZE-RACE-01): its decorations showed from two
sizes at once after a window resize under high CPU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/termio/Termio.zig | 11 ++++++++---
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/termio/Termio.zig b/src/termio/Termio.zig
index 33830032c..27d4d368a 100644
--- a/src/termio/Termio.zig
+++ b/src/termio/Termio.zig
@@ -527,9 +527,6 @@ pub fn resize(
     self.size = size;
     const grid_size = size.grid();
 
-    // Update the size of our pty.
-    try self.backend.resize(grid_size, size.terminal());
-
     // Enter the critical area that we want to keep small
     {
         self.renderer_state.mutex.lockUncancelable(global.io());
@@ -548,6 +545,14 @@ pub fn resize(
             },
         );
 
+        // Update the size of our pty only now, while we still hold the
+        // lock the read thread parses output under. The child learns the
+        // new size (SIGWINCH) once the grid already has it, so the frame it
+        // draws for that size is never parsed onto the old grid, and then
+        // moved by the resize: a full screen app's rows shifted, or wrapped
+        // and scrolled, under load.
+        try self.backend.resize(grid_size, size.terminal());
+
         // If we have size reporting enabled we need to send a report.
         if (self.terminal.modes.get(.in_band_size_reports)) {
             try self.sizeReportLocked(td, .mode_2048);
